    "case_id": "404",
    "case_summary": "A man in his late 70s presented with acute onset of redness, pain, and swelling of both lower limbs. The patient had received 4 cycles of chemotherapy with carboplatin, pemetrexed, and pembrolizumab for progressive disease and was currently in maintenance with pemetrexed-pembrolizumab. The edema appeared 5 days after the patient's 8th cycle of chemotherapy and evolved into sclerotic plaques.\n\nExamination revealed ill-defined, erythematoedematous plaques symmetrically distributed along the shins, with areas of induration. The patient remained afebrile and systemically well.\n\n- Laboratory tests: No elevation of inflammatory markers (normal white blood cell count and C-reactive protein level), with kidney, liver, and heart functions within normal ranges.- Imaging studies: Venous Doppler ultrasonography of legs showed no evidence of deep vein thrombosis.- Pathology:   Image Title: Hematoxylin-eosin–stained specimen from left shin  Image Description: Shows normal-appearing epidermis, thickened dermal collagen with activated fibroblasts, and lymphocytic perivascular infiltration.- Clinical Photography:  Image Title: Clinical image of lower limbs  Image Description: Ill-defined erythematoedematous plaques with induration symmetrically distributed on the distal two-thirds of the shins.",
